#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151837 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152055 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ffdfb29-a3d2-5a7a-868c-21eab8965922" :knowledge-point/title "Modular Residues of Positive Integers"}] :question/id #uuid "c165bfd1-f6cc-5849-b519-f3763e481cbd" :question/math-academy-id "e-14583" :question/problem "What is $26 \\textrm{ mod } 3?$" :question/worked-solution "The residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is the integer $0 \\leq r <  3$ such that\n\n$$\n26 \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,3).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $26$ as an integer multiple of $3,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $26 \\equiv 2 \\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,3)$ because\n\n$$\n26 = 8\\cdot 3 + {\\color{blue}{2}} .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the subtraction property. Remember that any integer multiple of $3$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}26 & ≡26-0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\  & ≡26-8\\cdot 3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\  & ≡26-24 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\  & ≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151838 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152056 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e70cef00-bf25-5423-85ac-2d3a44c68f35" :knowledge-point/title "Modular Residues of Negative Integers"}] :question/id #uuid "6f664a47-49c5-5183-bdcc-42ba86553a67" :question/math-academy-id "e-14584" :question/problem "What is $-13 \\textrm{ mod } 4?$" :question/worked-solution "The residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is the integer $0 \\leq r < 4$ such that\n\n$$\n-13 \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $-13$ as an integer multiple of $4,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $-13 \\equiv 3 \\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$ because\n\n$$\n-13 = (-4)\\cdot 4 + {\\color{blue}{3}} .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is ${\\color{blue}{3}}.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the addition property. Remember that any integer multiple of $4$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-13 & ≡-13+0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡-13+4\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡-13+16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is ${\\color{blue}{3}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151857 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152057 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a090bd2-cdb1-5680-81bc-3cb36de126c4" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Last Few Digits of a Number"}] :question/id #uuid "895dcb35-e68c-5b67-8f8a-8bc895bbde8f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14611" :question/problem "Let $a$ be a positive integer. Construct an expression that gives the last digit of $24a+15.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $b=24a+15.$ Note that $b$ is positive. Now, let $d_3,$ $d_2,$ and $d_1$ be the last three digits of the number $b{:}$ \n\n$$\nb = \\ldots d_3 d_2 d_1\n$$\n\nThen, $b$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =⋯+100{d}_{3}+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} \\\\  & =10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $b$ modulo $10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0\\cdot (⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $d_1$ is the last digit of $b.$\n\nFinally, since $b = 24a+15,$ and \n\n$$\n24\\equiv 4, \\qquad 15\\equiv 5 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,10)\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 24a+15 & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 4a+5 & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression that gives the last digit of $24a+15$ is the following:\n\n$$\n(4a+5) \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 10\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152055 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ffdfb29-a3d2-5a7a-868c-21eab8965922" :knowledge-point/title "Modular Residues of Positive Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3339da0a-421f-5923-8220-0d40e5fd564c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412282} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0f9a90-f1d4-5ec0-95b4-48df57d2cbe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412305} {:answer/id #uuid "a11bb931-7d3d-55b5-b9c9-3767270b03bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412310} {:answer/id #uuid "d6be7669-e478-5e84-a892-5c0f1ec270f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412328} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a6f499-512e-573b-8f2a-505f318cb0fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b0f9a90-f1d4-5ec0-95b4-48df57d2cbe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412305} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c08d870-0669-5e1c-afb0-29f7f768fa70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44155916-7397-5b39-a575-6db603bf8071" :question/math-academy-id "q-102405" :question/problem "$39\\text{ mod }4=$" :question/worked-solution "The residue of $39$ modulo $4$ is the integer $0\\le r<4$ such that\n\n$$\n39≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $39$ as an integer multiple of $4,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $39≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$ because\n\n$$\n39=9\\cdot 4+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $39$ modulo $4$ is $3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the subtraction property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}39 & ≡39-0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡39-9\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡39-36 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $39$ modulo $4$ is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152057 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a090bd2-cdb1-5680-81bc-3cb36de126c4" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Last Few Digits of a Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04db3f48-6af3-50ad-b665-4fb2472e5c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412263} {:answer/id #uuid "0655b49a-db5a-59a7-be19-739cf6bc3c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412265} {:answer/id #uuid "3fff7f15-6f97-5291-9721-ed269a080c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412288} {:answer/id #uuid "a8167e20-ebdc-5271-a290-e1a451315091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04db3f48-6af3-50ad-b665-4fb2472e5c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412263} :answer-field/id #uuid "67984a06-18d9-553b-84b4-5f0264f4bf20" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412356} {:ans